course: dictionary
lesson: 659
title: Bagpipes and where they come from
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A bagpipe is a musical instrument. The player fills a bag with air and squeezes it, and the air goes through pipes that make a loud, buzzing sound. Bagpipes are played at weddings, parades and funerals, and you can learn to play them in music lessons.
explain: Scotland is the place most people think of first, and the bagpipes there are a proud part of Scottish tradition. A bagpipe player is called a piper. You can say "play the bagpipes" or "a bagpipe player".
explain: Context helps you here too. «The trick is not to squeeze new things into old boxes» means you should not force a new instrument into a box you already know. «Bagpipes are not only Scottish» tells you that bagpipes are played in many countries, not just one.
fact: A bagpipe is a musical instrument with a bag that the player fills with air and squeezes.
fact: The pipes of a bagpipe make a loud, buzzing sound.
fact: A bagpipe player is called a piper.
fact: «Many places have bagpipes»: Scotland, Ireland's uilleann pipes, the gaita of Galicia in Spain and the gaida of Bulgaria.
fact: Bagpipes are played at weddings, parades and funerals, and they show Scottish tradition.
exercise: What is a bagpipe? | A tall drum played with sticks | *A musical instrument with a bag you squeeze and pipes that buzz | A bag with a zip, used for carrying books | A sweet, eaten at weddings
exercise: What sound do bagpipes make? | *A loud, buzzing sound | Silence until the end of the tune | A soft tinkle like a bell | A squeaky whistle
exercise: Which country's pipes are called the uilleann pipes? | Spain | Bulgaria | Italy | *Ireland
test: In which country is the gaida played? | Ireland | Spain | Scotland | *Bulgaria
test: What does «bagpipes are not only Scottish» mean? | Bagpipes are a kind of Scottish food | Bagpipes are only played in Scotland | *Bagpipes are played in other countries too | Scottish people do not like bagpipes
exam: Using the sentence «The trick is not to squeeze new things into old boxes», what should you do with a new instrument? | *Learn what it is, without forcing it into an old idea | Say it is just a guitar or a harp | Throw it away because it is strange | Squeeze it into the box of an instrument you already know
